1
00:00:00,190 --> 00:00:05,140
In September 1929 the Dow closed
at three hundred and eighty-one.

2
00:00:05,190 --> 00:00:08,373
It didn't close that high
again for twenty-five years.

3
00:00:08,633 --> 00:00:11,873
Put the market on a track
of its own. Black Thursday,

4
00:00:11,923 --> 00:00:15,503
Black Monday, Black Tuesday,
and that was only the start:

5
00:00:15,553 --> 00:00:18,653
it kept falling for nearly
three years, to forty-one.

6
00:00:18,703 --> 00:00:20,560
Eighty-nine percent gone.

7
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:23,560
Then the climb back: twenty-two
years, through the New Deal,

8
00:00:23,610 --> 00:00:28,298
a world war, and into the
fifties. November 1954.

9
00:00:28,648 --> 00:00:32,838
That's the price alone. Count the
dividends, and the fall in prices,

10
00:00:32,888 --> 00:00:37,528
and someone who bought at the
top was even by about 1936.

11
00:00:37,578 --> 00:00:39,316
The timeline's note says so.

12
00:00:39,536 --> 00:00:43,606
The whole Depression is on this
timeline, every date with its source.

13
00:00:43,656 --> 00:00:45,800
Free, on OpenChronology.
